# The install lives in this directory now — the clone is the install root — so
# these ignore rules are what stands between a mail password and `git add -A`.
# They are anchored with a leading slash on purpose: an unanchored `hermes/`
# would swallow tracked code, and an unanchored `env` would match any file of
# that name anywhere in the tree.
#
# Ignoring is a convention, not a boundary. Two things back it up:
#   - scripts/install.sh refuses to write if any of these is tracked or would
#     show up in `git status`. It checks before it writes, not after.
#   - scripts/test_paths.sh asserts these rules in CI, so a rule cannot be
#     dropped without a test going red.
#
# And one thing it does not protect against: `git clean -xdf` deletes ignored
# files, which here means the mailbox password, both route secrets, the roster
# and the UID baseline, in one command. Use `git clean -df` on a live install.

# Mailbox credentials.
/.env

# Generated by the installer, owned by the installer.
/runtime.env
/install.manifest

# The two Hermes route secrets. Anchored so HERMES.md, harness/adapters/hermes.py
# and examples/hermes-routes.yaml stay tracked.
/hermes/

# Queue state, cursors, delivery status and logs.
/state/

# The real roster is per-install: it says who this agent may contact
# unattended, and a git pull must never be able to change that list.
/roster.md

*.log
__pycache__/
